---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A one rep max is the heaviest load you could lift once with good form, and it is the number
      strength programs are written against. This 1RM calculator, or max lift calculator, estimates
      it from a submaximal set
      you actually completed, running the Epley, Brzycki, Lombardi, and O'Conner formulas at once and
      averaging them. The Epley formula multiplies the weight by 1 plus reps divided by 30, so 100 kg
      for 5 reps estimates about 117 kg; the brzycki formula divides by a falling linear term and
      reads slightly lower. The result also converts straight into the
      training percentage table, from 95 percent for doubles down to 65 percent for sets of fifteen,
      so progressive overload needs no arithmetic.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The One Rep Max Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How the Estimate Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      There is a stable relationship between how heavy a load is relative to your maximum and how
      many times you can move it. Roughly 90 percent of a max allows four reps, 80 percent allows
      eight, 70 percent allows twelve. Every estimation formula is a curve fitted to that
      relationship, run backwards: given the weight and the reps, what maximum would produce that
      pairing. This is why the reliability of the answer depends entirely on the set being taken
      close to failure. A comfortable set of eight describes a different curve than a brutal one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="formulas">
    <h2>Epley Versus Brzycki, and Why Four Formulas <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      How the estimation formulas are derived is the same story four times: each is a curve fitted
      to the reps-versus-load relationship, then run backwards.
      Epley uses a linear rep term, Brzycki divides by a falling linear term, and Lombardi and
      O'Conner fit different curves again. At three to five reps they agree within a couple of
      percent, so the choice is irrelevant. At twelve or more they can diverge by ten percent or
      more, because a long set is limited by muscular endurance rather than by peak force, and
      endurance varies enormously between lifters. Showing the spread rather than one number is the
      honest presentation: when the formulas disagree, that disagreement is information about how far
      you extrapolated.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="training-percentages">
    <h2>Turning a Max Into Working Weights <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Programs are written in percentages because they scale to any lifter. What percentage of a
      max suits each training goal is well established: strength work generally
      sits at 85 percent and above for one to five reps, hypertrophy at 70 to 85 percent for six to
      twelve, and muscular endurance below 70 percent. The percentage table in the result does the
      conversion for you, so five sets of five at 80 percent becomes a bar weight rather than a
      calculation. Re-estimate every few weeks as the submaximal set gets easier and the whole table
      moves with it.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Estimate from three to five reps">
      <p>Low-rep sets barely extrapolate, so the estimate is reliable, and the set is still safe to perform. Above ten reps the formulas model the wrong quality and the answer drifts. A hard set of four beats a heavier set you cut short.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p><strong>100 kg for 5 reps.</strong> For example, Epley gives 117 kg and Brzycki 113 kg, an average near 115, so a program calling for 80 percent means about 92 kg.</p>
    <p><strong>60 kg for 3 reps.</strong> For example, the four formulas land between 64 and 66 kg, a spread of under 4 percent, which is the tightest range you can get from an estimate.</p>
    <p><strong>80 kg for 12 reps.</strong> For example, Brzycki reads 115 kg and Epley 112 kg, but a 15 rep set from the same lifter might imply 120, which is the drift a high-rep estimate carries.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Estimating from a set you stopped early">
      <p>
        Reps left in reserve make the estimate too low, and the whole percentage table then
        undershoots. If you could have made two more, the input weight and reps describe a lighter
        effort than your true capacity. Take the set close to failure or accept a conservative answer.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Attempting the estimate straight away">
      <p>
        The number says what the arithmetic expects, not what your joints and technique will produce
        today. Work up in singles across several sets, use safety bars or a spotter, and stop when
        bar speed collapses or form breaks.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Best on the big lifts">
      <p>Squat, bench press, deadlift, and overhead press behave predictably. On isolation work and on lifts where balance fails before the muscle does, the rep-to-load relationship is much noisier. Everything here runs in your browser and never leaves your device.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
